title: list() cannot paginate and drops the list's metadata (resourceVersion, continue)
updated: 2026-10-18T06:35:22Z
---

`list` takes only `namespace`, `labelSelector` and `fieldSelector` (lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:1884) - `limit`, `continue` and `resourceVersion` croak as unknown arguments - and `_inflate_list` builds the result from the items alone (`IO::K8s::List->new(items => \@objects, item_class => $class)`, line 1611), although IO::K8s::List has a `metadata` attribute for exactly this.

```
perl -Ilib -MKubernetes::REST -MKubernetes::REST::HTTPResponse -e 'my $api=Kubernetes::REST->new(server=>{endpoint=>"https://x"},credentials=>{token=>"t"},resource_map_from_cluster=>0); my $l=$api->inflate_list("Pod",Kubernetes::REST::HTTPResponse->new(status=>200,content=>q({"kind":"PodList","apiVersion":"v1","metadata":{"resourceVersion":"4711","continue":"abc"},"items":[]}))); print defined $l->metadata ? "present" : "undef", "\n"'
# undef
```

Consequences: a large collection can only be fetched in one response (the API server's own chunking, `limit` + `continue`, is unreachable), and the collection resourceVersion is lost, so the list-then-watch pattern - the clean fix discussed for k69 and for Net::Async::Kubernetes k73 - cannot be built on the public API. The watch POD's own 410 recipe lists and then has to set `$rv = undef` for that reason (line 2990-2994). Net::Async::Kubernetes `list` has the same argument list and goes through the same `inflate_list`.

Done when: `inflate_list` hands the response's `metadata` to IO::K8s::List, `list` accepts `limit`, `continue` and `resourceVersion` (sent as query parameters), the POD shows a paging loop and list-then-watch, and Net::Async::Kubernetes follows (its allowed-argument list).

